 ### [Vector-Matrix (VM) Decomposition](3452-vector-matrix-vm-decomposition.md)
-Vector-Matrix (VM) decomposition is a tensor factorization technique that relaxes the low-rank constraints for two modes of a tensor by factorizing it into compact vector and matrix factors, effectively acting as a special case of Block Term Decomposition (BTD). Unlike [CP Decomposition](2255-cp-decomposition.md), which uses a sum of outer products of pure vectors, VM uses a sum of vector-matrix outer products. This allows each component to express more complex tensor data of higher ranks, significantly reducing the required number of components compared to CP while maintaining a memory complexity of $O(N^2)$ compared to the $O(N^3)$ of dense grids.
+A variant that relaxes low-rank constraints for two modes, using vector-matrix outer products instead of pure vector outer products. It reduces the required number of components $R$ compared to CP for the same expression capacity, at the cost of larger per-component size. Choose VM when CP requires too many components for complex scenes, leading to high computational costs.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Training time increases with the number of components $R$ [S1]. Reconstruction speed is generally faster than dense grids but slower than VM decomposition for the same quality [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity**: Space complexity is reduced from $O(n^3)$ to $O(n)$ compared to direct voxel grid optimization, where $n$ is the grid resolution dimension [S1][S3, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance**: For a 300x300x300 feature grid with P=27 channels, TensoRF-CP with R=192 uses 360K parameters, achieving 0.048% compression rate compared to 756M parameters in a dense grid [S1]. TensoRF with CP decomposition achieves reconstruction in less than 30 minutes with a model size smaller than 4 MB [S3, unofficial]. On the Synthetic-NeRF dataset, CP-384 achieves PSNR 31.56/31.23 (w/ L1 / w/o L1), SSIM 0.949/0.947, LPIPS 0.076/0.078 [S1].
-- **Strengths**: Significant memory reduction, fast reconstruction compared to MLP-based NeRF [S3, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Requires many components for complex scenes, leading to high computational costs [S1][S3, unofficial]. Less expressive than VM decomposition for the same number of components [S1].
-- **When to Use**: When memory is constrained and scenes are not overly complex, or as a baseline for tensorial radiance fields [G].
-- **When Not to Use**: When high-quality reconstruction of complex scenes is required and computational resources are limited, VM decomposition is preferred [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   **Space:** Reduces space complexity from $O(n^3)$ (for a dense grid of resolution $n$) to $O(n \cdot R)$ or $O(n)$ if $R$ is considered constant relative to $n$ [S2][S4][S6, unofficial]. For a 300x300x300 grid with $P=27$ channels, CP with $R=192$ uses 360K parameters, achieving a 0.048% compression rate compared to 756M parameters in a dense grid [S2].
+*   **Time:** Reconstruction quality improves with more iterations, but higher $R$ increases training time [S2]. TensoRF with CP achieves reconstruction in less than 30 minutes with a model size < 4MB [S6, unofficial].
+
+**Performance:**
+*   On the D-NeRF dataset with $R=384$, CP Decomposition achieves PSNR 30.302, SSIM 0.959, LPIPS 0.059, and Training Time 13m 06s [S3].
+*   On Synthetic-NeRF, VM-48 outperforms CP-384 in PSNR (32.39 vs 31.56) and SSIM (0.957 vs 0.949) with significantly fewer components [S2].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Extremely compact memory footprint; no neural networks required for feature storage; fast reconstruction compared to MLP-based NeRF [S2][S6, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High compactness can require many components ($R$) to model complex scenes, leading to high computational costs in training and potential overfitting if $R$ is too large [S2][S6, unofficial]. Unlike Tucker, CP often requires larger factor matrices ($R_n \approx I_n$) to represent the dataset effectively [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-CP Decomposition, also known as CANDECOMP/PARAFAC, is a classical tensor decomposition method [S2, unofficial]. In the context of volume rendering, it was introduced in the TensoRF paper (Chen et al., 2022) as a method to factorize radiance fields into compact vectors [S1][S3, unofficial]. It builds on the idea of using tensor factorization for efficient scene representation, offering a low-rank regularization in the optimization process [S1].
+CP Decomposition, also known as CANDECOMP/PARAFAC, was introduced by [7] (Kruskal, 1977; Carrol and Chang, 1970) and is a special case of the Tucker model [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in chemometrics, psychometrics, and signal processing. In computer graphics and volume rendering, it was adapted for radiance field modeling by TensoRF [S2][S6, unofficial] and HexPlane [S3] to compress 3D/4D feature grids.
 
